The utility is a text‐based frontend program for interacting with
It is used to query current status, change configuration, trigger
events, and request interactive user input.  The utility can show
the  current authentication status, selected security mode, dot11
and dot1x MIBs, etc.  In addition, can configure EAPOL state  ma‐
chine  parameters  and  trigger  events such as reassociation and
IEEE 802.1X logoff/logon.  The utility provides an  interface  to
supply  authentication  information such as username and password
when it is not provided in the configuration file.  This  can  be
used, for example, to implement one‐time passwords or generic to‐
ken  card  authentication  where the authentication is based on a
challenge‐response that uses an external  device  for  generating
the  response.   The  utility supports two modes: interactive and
command line.  Both modes share the same command set and the main
difference is that in interactive mode, provides access to  unso‐
licited  messages  (event  messages, username/password requests).
Interactive mode is started when is executed without any  parame‐
ters  on  the  command  line.  Commands are then entered from the
controlling terminal in response to the prompt.  In command  line
mode,  the  same  commands are entered as command line arguments.
The control interface of can be configured to allow non‐root user
access by using the parameter in the  configuration  file.   This
makes  it possible to run with a normal user account.  When needs
authentication parameters, such as username  and  password,  that
are  not  present  in  the configuration file, it sends a request
message to all attached frontend programs, e.g.,  in  interactive
mode.   The  utility shows these requests with a prefix, where is
or (one‐time password), is a unique identifier  for  the  current
network,  and  is  description  of the request.  In the case of a
(One Time Password) request, it includes the challenge  from  the
authentication  server.  A user must supply the needed parameters
in response  to  these  requests.   For  example,  CTRL‐REQ‐PASS‐
WORD‐1:Password needed for SSID foobar
 password 1 mysecretpassword

